# React Native Paper first

When building or editing UI in this Expo app, **prefer `react-native-paper` components** whenever they cover the use case, instead of raw React Native primitives or ad-hoc styling.

## Default choices

| Need | Prefer | Avoid when Paper fits |
|------|--------|------------------------|
| Text | `Text` from `react-native-paper` (variants) | `Text` from `react-native` for app chrome |
| Buttons | `Button`, `IconButton`, `FAB` | `Pressable` + styled `Text` for standard actions |
| Surfaces / cards | `Surface`, `Card` | Plain `View` with manual elevation/shadow |
| Text fields | `TextInput` from Paper (outlined / flat) | `TextInput` from `react-native` for forms unless Paper blocks behavior |
| Dialogs / modals | `Dialog`, `Portal`, `Modal` from Paper where appropriate | Custom full-screen `Modal` only when Paper layout is insufficient |
| Lists / menus | `List.*`, `Menu`, `Divider`, `Chip`, `Badge` | Reimplementing the same patterns |
| Feedback | `Snackbar`, `Banner`, `ActivityIndicator` (Paper) | One-off toast stacks |

## Theme

- Respect the app’s `PaperProvider` / `paperTheme` integration: use Paper components so **colors, elevation, and roundness** stay aligned with the design system.

## Exceptions

- Use React Native or other libraries when Paper **does not support** the interaction, platform API, or layout (e.g. some maps, camera, or highly custom gestures).
- **Performance-sensitive** lists may still use `FlatList` / `SectionList` from RN; compose rows with Paper `List.Item` or themed cells as needed.

## Imports

- Import from `react-native-paper` in one line per file convention already used in the project (e.g. `import { Button, Text, Card } from 'react-native-paper'`).
